  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Goltzius made drawings on-site and then made engravings after his designs once he returned to Haarlem in 1591. He portrayed the Farnese Hercules from a low viewpoint to capture the awesome experience of first encountering the famous monument. Goltzius showed the sculpture from behind and in shadow, emphasizing its glowing highlights.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "title": "The Farnesian Hercules",
          "publisher": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "readAt": "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/523229",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 4,
          "license": "CC0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Hendrick Goltzius was one of many late Renaissance artists who felt compelled to travel to Italy as part of his artistic training. He went with one purpose—to study antique sculpture.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "title": "The Farnesian Hercules",
          "publisher": "Cleveland Museum of Art",
          "readAt": "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/523229",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 4,
          "license": "CC0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Many engravings and woodcuts spread the fame of the Farnese's Hercules. … Before photography, prints were the only way to put the image into many hands.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Farnese Hercules",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farnese_Hercules",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 5,
